      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>LADTES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADTES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>Just at present some very pretty frocks are coming to the front for girls, and of the many you will have seen is a rather elaborate frock for a girl of eight, made in fawn poplin, the skirt plain, and the bodice boasting ot a yoke of white satin and lace, while the poplin in its turn is edged with a ruching of white satin ribbon. The waistband is of white satin, finishing at the sides in front with a rosette and at the back with Paßh ends. This is very pretty. indeed, and gives quite a charming style to the figure.</p>
        <p>The millinery to be seen now the weattiee has begun to brighten up a little is simply gorgeous. One hat, for instance, is in white straw of the sailor shape, the crown hidden by a thick ruching of white tulle rising somewhat higher at the left side to fOrm a soft be.ekground to some temptingly natural cherries. The brim is slightly raised at one aide to show a group of pretty pink roees underneath, which, when on the bead, give the hat that fashionable upward tilt,and the same model can he copied in any colour deeired very cheaply. While I was admiring that one, I was attracted by its striking neighbetir, a red straw almost covered with scarlet poppies, intermixed with ears of wheat and two shade° of soft silk ril.bee, while another was eqnally attractive by reason of its dainty blending of colours. Here the brim was made of guipure lace, a fineness being given to it by en edging of straw correspenoing to that of the crown, the colour of which was somewhat, of r6.-bda a tone.. The trimming consisted of large ribbon rosettes in shades of redid% and pale bloe, groups of green poppy buds, a quantity of oats, and also a paste buckle placed in the eentre ot a ribbon rosette. Another exceedinOy charming toque which is well worth going to feee, ie c.turn- posed of sequined net, sparkling with gold. the brim pushed back on one side to give place to a full rosette of delicate blue ribbon, forming a sweet contrast to the pink roses trailing partly over the crown and brim; lastly, a waving hunch of black osprey completes this very pretty toque.</p>
        <p>Turning from the subplot of hate to the question of blouses, I must netnews that there het s not been, with the prevailing weether, the last few days, and especially during Whit-week much chance of exhibiting the latest styles. Otie pretty one I noticed was made of blue glace silk, finely tucked on the bodice and sleeves, and below the yoke is a deep band of creamy guipure lafs.s in a pointed design, which is mounted oc white silk. The back of the blouse is trimmed in the same way, and it fastens invisibly. Another pretty one in blue glach silk is trimmed with ornaments of creamy guipure lace ou the yoke and at the wrists. Less elaborate blouses in various styles can be all colours, and smart silk shirts also.</p>
        <p>Blouses can be decorated most delightfully now with the drawing braid, and those of silk are surprisingly cheap. A grey voile, tritium:ld with a deeper grey and white braid, arranged in cunning little trefoils, and with the 84110 braid on the white revers, is extremely smart, and there are superb possibilities for bo,tting and yachting gowns. Fir those who admire voile, but do not wish a Bilk foundation, the substitute is a tine nun's veiling, which hangs in the same fashion, but the coarser type of veiling is used solely for lining. There is a P.m4rt little gown to be seen marle of store-hue &amp;Palinode clutii, trinpued with , f tncy braid iii dark blue and whi!e. The skirt cut, in send-circular fa,hion with a gored front, and is arranged in flat pleats at the btek. The ilt,ou coat has spade ends and short revers, and turn-down collar, which does not meet in front. A full biome vest of tucked muslin awl jtbit 07 lace is iasteuod , with a folded stock round the neck, which fastens at the side. This is a pretty model for cloth or cashmere, and is eff.cLive in black, with black awl white braid, and a white vest. The tunic is extremely fa,hionable, and ins.ny open in the centre and describe 11.ng points, but the "lily" shape, perfectly plain at the top, and cut in rounded scallops at the edge, is an e.-peeial favourite, and is nearly as long a the ordinary. short washing skirt, arid a full fluted skirt falls below it. Tois is a heeidning nolo, as shorter ones cut up the figure and detract from the heights.. No fulness whatever is yet shcmn in the top of tee skirt, but with the approich of winter we 'hail doubtless see some more ample style intro. duce&amp;</p>
        <p>Your loving JIENNIZ</p>
