        <p>BRILLIA, 1 tsULTION.</p>
        <p>The marriage of Sir Peter Carlaw Walker, Bart, of Osmaston %tenor. Derbyshire, and head of the well-known firm of Peter Walker and Sotoof Liver- pool, Warrtng,o t, and Burton-on erent, with Miss Etnel s.rite)yer, fifth daughtor ot Me 11.0. and tte Hon. Mre.Okeever, of Oeeover Hall, Ashb urne, wee celebrated on l'usisdey in the little church at Oke- over, and Was male tee OCCithen of a s rising dem instration of the esteem in whic.) the hr de gromn and the bride's family are held in the neighheurh ell. The man.-ion and toe church are so close to etch olier thet the hricie'is procession had to peas but a few yards —across. the carriage drive, in fact—to reach the edifice. The church would have been quite insuffipient to aeconamodate the large number of guests entertained by Mr. and Mrs. Oki-toyer, to say no fling of the gr ;at, crowds of country people who diro-d all the approached to the detnesne, and places alone could be found for the immediate relatives and friends. The interilr had been -quietly but mo-t effectively decorated,t ie porch with rows of White, purple, and yellow pause s on a ground of moas,anci the chancel with palms,,, white rodoiendrons, and ! arum lilies; whilst variegated ivy was entwined ; roan() the altar rails. The tal palms vittre not; :owed to conceal the faucoes Ukeeter b-aeseis, whin rank amoligst the finest specimens of art work in the kin'-in, and which have an interest- ing history. They peeeed into the poo,e,t@irm of the Okeovera in the y. ar 1447. having been required by them from the Z.LteUes. When the little n.urch was rest ored under the euperviaion Of Sir GI leer Scott, in 1858, the brae:este were kto!en. asetaae Set of vandaliqn, as it weuld have been QUIL“ lifl- p-s'ibla to dispose of them under any eireum.ntnct:s. The thief was evidently of the seine opin on, at, some time alt-rwards a small portion of them were div.oveted in a well at A-hbourne, but the re- mainder have never been found to this day. Though somewhat hazy and overcast the morning was beau t folly fine, aril the sun shone pleasantly though Hy no menu brilliantly at inrervale thrQugtr_mt the day The bridal procession left th? fLit shortly trim* noon , —the bride, cf course, escorted by her father, and at- tended by seven lforidesmsldr—Mias Victoria Okeover ,('ier sister), Miss Evelyn Croft, Miss Dorothy Cour,and Miss Doris Walker (rltere ot tie bridegroom). Miss Clowes (cousin of the Uris e). Miss tleits, and Mist, Ethel Gillett. The bride, whose t.rdis IN aa borne by little Miss Cynthia Allsepp (her nieie )sat Master Vihcent Walker ! (nephew of he thilrgro ,m), wore a lovely bidet g we of rich ivory duchesse ratite ex, risitely embroidered with pearl and silver, anti draped with fl aunt et of beau- tiful Brussels lace. A scarf of the sante lace was at- ta h el to the left -shoulder with a cluster of orauee corn, and draped the entire length of the train. A lat-e veil ornamented weiti pear s was Mao worn. Tne brides-1 maids' gowns; consisted of -watbed bodices ci!ivory fancy uk gauzk, with rucks!' Calba crieuww,t-s and tarapery across the her, haugieg in c,s,;ades at the left side. , The skirts had poOited tunics, the bat k and front being of fancy eacz,. finished with two chiffon ruches and shaped cliffon flounces edged with rut ha Tne underskirts were of blue t ffe a silk, and the cliff an sashes at the bark ivere thdetted vete ruchiegs. bridems d.' hate Were of blue satin straw, with white crenolinseand teimmed wi• h a-mai:ran feathers. They were ba Iced up hien on true one side. with rues ant fie m the other the fashionable tides scarf depended, and was t rich don to the dress. The bridesmaids wore shamrock br ,oches in lurquo se md diamonds, the gifts of the bridegroom. and c..rre d bonnets of pink Carnations. Ma CyntLir Allsopp, as train bearer, wore a snout fr ck of ivory sa in, with vaneykes of chiffon inuret the skirt, and a butyl-es collar tied by a sash utter her arms. The Hon. Moo Okerer-r wee dressed in purple v lyct over yeilow k tr mmed with point de Ven as Lice and isvel paste- manterie. Her bonnet was 0, pert*. II 'were with smitten plume, and . jewels. Miea Okeover's chess • was of Pervanche milk trimmed vital insertion c team lace applique. Miss Ruth Ckeever wore e real lace lobe over rose coloursd chiffon arid Alice silk. Toe H a Mrs. Assheton Cie.z an, another of the Wile's sist r, wore mahraison pink silk trimm..d -with point d'esprit lice, beautifully embr idee I ni!h true loverte heot and shamrvek.- The H II o ber! Ailsepp,al,.o a sister,. wore turquoise p ule sole tile,trinuned wilt lace chaffon Bounces and Hon Mrs. Lore Phlltps wore a mauve voile dr,,es wti silk embroidery. There is tea vicar in O's °over in the men arc' se of the term, The living is One of the very tee Is Eaglanc- known as a donaiay,,, and Mr. Okerlr r titre 0,0, :.pfsco pal antlicrity, and is, in tact, hie own bishop. But this, of course, is only one of the meet. cations, vie -wieed reminders one meets with at Okmever, wheo. by the way, the family of 'he name have been in roomful and uninterrupted poise sion for close upon a thowiand years. In the absence of the vicar of the, parish toe wedding ceremony- was perfumed by the Rev. T. Anchitel Anson, rector of Lorreforcl, Derty, tie vener- able great uncle of the b ide, tht, Bev. John Young, rector of the adjoining parish of Biore, a,,d the Rev L. Nieholson, vicar of Osmaston. The usual weedieg hymns were snag during the sovica, and the bride was rises awey by her lather. Mr. Nugent Howard, of Broughton Hall, Malpas, Cheshire, acted in the capacity of beet man, and as Sir Peter and Lady auker left the church the ergaiiiis; played Mendeis- aohnes Wedding March. A reception was afterwards bed the bah, at which a very large number of gue.ts were entertained, and shortly before-Mr:4e o'clock Sir Pi tar and L-dy Walker left the 'mansion for Ashbourne Station, wh ther they were escorted by a iquadron of rtrystdre YeomeoryCavalry, in which 