      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>et oT the Otil Lancers cured of Ger23umption.</head>
        <p>A et oT the Otil Lancers cured of Ger23umption.</p>
        <p>EX.S. REGLINT HAnny COLLINS. of 3, Turner-street, Cornhrook, Man- chester, is forty-three, and looks the very picture of health and happiness. "Some account of my experiences ? " he commenced. "Well, I served f r twenty-one ye,trs in the 9th Lancers, and left that regiment as a sergeant in 1891. During my Service I was for three years IN AFGHANISTNN 'UNDER LORD ROIIERTS,</p>
        <p>anti was awarded three medals. Immediately after leaving the army I joined the Corps of Commissionaires; I was kept on door duty for about three years, and during that time I was contracting one cold ou top of another until I got so bad-that I had to be removed to work inside." "The three years outside work had made an impression upon your health I suppose?" queried the reporter of the South Manchester Chronicle. "Yes it had," replied the ot-sergeaut. "I began to spit phlegm with streaks of blood, my breath got bail, I lost my appetite, and I got so weak that I could only walk slowly. I lost a stone in weight, and every other night I awoke wet with perspiration. I went to see two very able doctors, who told me I was in the first stage of consumption, and</p>
        <p>I WOULD NOT LAST LONG. - I was by this time oblig.d to lie in bed. Whilst laid up I read of a marvellous cure by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. The cam was exactly similar to mine, and thei symptoms appeared to be the same. I sent my wife for a box of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, and after having taken the first box I felt slightly better. After I had taken a second box I went to work again. I continued taking the pills, and after another eight boxes I was completely cured, and my health at present is very good. I told the doctor that Dr. Williams' Pink Pills had saved my life.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>An Ex. Marine cured or Xmligestion and Gastric Ulcer.</head>
        <p>An Ex. Marine cured or Xmligestion and Gastric Ulcer.</p>
        <p>Mr:. HERBY PEERS of Wednesfield, near Wolverhampton, a bronzed, healthy-looking man of decided soldierly appearance, is fifty-three, and has served twenty-three and a-half years in the Royal Navy. He enlisted in the Marines in 1863, served on 11-11. S. Penelope at the.bombardment of Alexandria, and in 1885 and 1886 was in Egypt FIGHTING AT SUAKIN</p>
        <p>against Osman Digna. He hold 3, a medal and bar for the bombardment of Alexandria, and a bar for.Suakin. At the close of the campaign he came home invalided. left the service, and returned to Wednesfield to take up his trade of a keysmith. Three or four years ago he broke down in health, and underwent an operation. His health, however, was not restored, and doctors said he was still suffering from gastric ulcer. "I vomited everything I ate," said Mr. Peers, "and lost weight, until I became like a shadow. I had had two doctors treating me when my brother first told me about Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. I bought a box at once, and commenced to take them. From that time I began to get better. Before I could not eat any solid food, but after taking the pills I began to eat and enjoy food. I can hardly satisfy my appetite now. I attribute my recovery entirely to Dr. Williams' Pills. I can eat anything that is put before me."—Midland Weekly News.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>mAY VESN F '1..'.1-VAL AT</head>
        <p>mAY VESN F '1..'.1-VAL AT</p>
        <p>LYM</p>
        <p>The inhabitants of Lymtn may congratulate themselves on the fact that their anon .1 event, the Lymm Band of Hope end May Qtenn Fe•tivai, has been allowed to become—for this year—a thing of the past without the depre sing influence f rainy weather. Some xitliges have wad it) pcar pone this in- teresting event more than once iu consequence of wet weather, and in al- ri-sp,ct Lymm is fortun- ate. The weather has domin-i••ed everything lately, but last Thursday, the day in quest i n,—as they bay in the polio: court -tie wea! her elerktle-cended to threats and refrained from fulfilling them. The dry was of a dull, leaden hue at day, full of prism- bilities—an i as it bappened,unrealized m 03, charming Lym in, put. on tfrr best appear- ance on this occasion and lo,.ked—well, herself. By one o'clock the v:llage was simply alive with people from the country. round. Vi: or arrived by train 'hue, and wagonette, and let me net f3rgst it, the übi pitons cyclist was there in full force. All these people had (me c( IntrIOEI (:biect in view—the crown- ing of the May Qwen.. Deeptte the claims of busi- ness, and the demands on the time of Most of tis,it is pleasant to be able to forget our respons;biliti as for a time and throw ourselves comple, elv into the enjoyment of the moment. However, all this i; somewhat irrelevant, and the writer must realise his own reepon•ibilities. The day or more particularly the alterneon was recognised as a holiday by the shopkeepers and only the refreshment house keepere seemed to consider there was any necessity for keeping open. The crowds of sight-seers in- Creas-d and lined the streets, and about halfpast one o'clock the procession averalled at Lymm cross and proceeded by wry of Eagle Brow, Booth's Hill. New road, and returned to the fottball field by Slagle Brow. The procession was an extremely pretty spectacle, and submitted irself to the admir- ing gaze aid comments of the multitude in the foilowieg order . _</p>
        <p>The Birder (mounted), Mount.O sties de cpunp, Cape Ungineer, Central Indian Horse Lymrn Subscription BAR&amp; Congregational Hand ot Hope</p>
        <p>DRESS lfioC iSION". Oliver Cromwell (mounted). Royal Jubilee Colonisl Troopers and Officers. Benfral slhks. Bombay Lancers. Colonial G-boorkas, Madras Light Cavalry. Troops. New !:‘outh Vales Volunteers, Madras Engineers.</p>
        <p>Lyentu Church Bin., Circaedian Dancers.</p>
        <p>May-poie Dancers in e•laraeters. Cherry Hip., Little Boy Blue. Little Mist Muff, t. Plerott, 2 French Fishn. Girls, 2 Spanish T)ons, 2 Little Girls in Blue, Ohitte=e and Japanese, 2 Italian Girls 2 Brigands, Red Riclin, Bond Woodman.</p>
        <p>Colonial Officer,. KM( roopors on foot. Indian Light Cavalry, South kfrienn Horse, Bengal Lancers,. Ind!an.eavalry &amp;e.,</p>
        <p>Scotch Girls, Irishmen, Fl‘mer Girls, Fairy Queen. ReMason Crum', Mon Friday Wellington, Neleon, Milk Maidd, Gl.aners, Cavaliers, B.T Princes, America, Webb Girls, Nurses. Goblin, _ Jockey,</p>
        <p>Beef Eaters. Group of Courtiets, oetre flearer, Crown Hearers, Carriage containing MAY CrIFIRN, Miss rivain Bearers, Carriage containing Maids of Honour, Base Queen, Spring, Summer, Aututnn. Winter, Britannia. John Bull, Grace Darling. _</p>
        <p>Indian Chief—mounted. and Canadian Hnesv—mounted.</p>
        <p>The preparations vn the Reid for the ceremonies had been most complete, and therefore everything as arranged proceeded without a hitch. The Queen—Mida E. Li ah—looking extremely pretty, and fulfilling her office with grace and modesty, ascended the throne, trected at the far end of the field, attended by her maids of honours and cour- tiers and was duly crowned with all the eclat becoming to the occasion. After the emblem of her power in the shape of a sceptre had been pre-ented to her the maypole dance was proceeded with and was executed with exquisite grace. To many people. these after proceedings were the most enjoyable part of the whole affair. Proctor's Gymnasts (of Manchester) excited the admiiaton of the spectators with wonderful feats on hori- zontal bars and a fancy trick cyclist exhibited phenomenal skill on the wheel. The Circassian dance was also gone through by the young ladies, and later the intricacies of the Wash- ington Post dunce were s‘lely adventured, and probably owing to the pleasure with which the per- formance had been received,t he dance was repeated. The performers displared cleverness beyond praise, and the efforts of their tutors have been most worthily rewarded. The same dances and perform- ances were given in the evening, when the enter- tainment was continued and in addition the Lymm Church Baud and Lymm and Oughtrington Silver Band played capita) st feet ions, to give the onlookers of the afternoon an opportunity of tripping the light fantastic toe." We may mention that photo- graphs of the different proceedings were taken by Mr. Bradshaw, of Altrincharn, during the afternoon. To the following officials must the credit be laid for the complete succtsl of the event :--131entlemen's Committee: Chairman, Nev C E Watson; vice- chairman, Mr James Astley ; hon. secretary, Mr George Willan ; hon. tree-surer, Mr It H Gotobed ; and Messrs S Browning,J fl Holt, James hewitt, A Massey, H Simpson, T Noar and L L Noar.</p>
      </div>
