        <p>48, LORU-STREET,</p>
        <p>THE Unionist papers refer to• The Moral of Southport. the Southport victory as if it</p>
        <p>were a temporary mood that it indicated, or a mere personal tiibute to a popu- lar local man. It is probably a good deal more than these things. AL political victories are merely temporary, in the historical sense, as the victors of 1895 have to be frequently reminded,and Sir GEORGE PILKINGTON'S 10( Id claims did not avail him whim he was overthrown by that plausible young man who made S ,whport a stepping-stone to the Viceroyalty of India. We must look a little deeper to obtain an insight that will help us, and the hint of tne Standard, that some Unionists had been disconcerted and unsettled by the speeciaes of the FIRST LORD of the TREASURY, Will be fonud valuable. From the part played by Protestantism in the contest. we must turn to the figures to gauge the effect. There can be no doubt that this effect has been great, substantial, and decisive. If the attitude of the Government towards Mr. McArthur's bill had not been so hostile, so agnostic, so apologetic towards the Biala(); s, S )utliport would prob- ably have been lost to the Liberals,or won only by a bare majority. As it is, the figures are striking, and have been so regarded by all open-minded and candid politicians. The electors have given a ver- dict on the question of the hour—the recovery,the. safeguarding, the re-affirmation of Protestantism. The subsidiary questions were important,and they might have obscured this one if there had been less vitality in it, and less of the national spirit. The country will, apparently, decline to tolerate the balancing, undecided, sceptical attitude of Mr. Arthur Balfour and the other members of the Government. It will not sanction Romanising under the plea of religious liberty, unsatisfactory law, or tribunals that are unae-eptable to the 'High Church clergy, or Om necessity of preserv- ing the connection between Church and Sate. It will not allow any qu -stion of an Irish Parlia- ment to narcotise its intelligence. The danger is real and visible, and it must be guarded against. The" Times " hopes the acceptance of the deci- sions of the Archbishop will avert danger, but they are about, it seems, to give jmigment which may not be in accord with the legal decision al- ready pronounced by a duly constituted court of the realm. Incense is illegal, and it has been so adjudged. Can the Archbishops alter the law ? Will their sophistry about it net bring the law into more contempt than it is held in at present ? How can the Arch- bishops relieve the clergy of the Church from obedience to the law of the land? It seems to us that ease and peace, produced by tactics of this kind, are much more injarious to Protestantisna than open rebellion. The electors will have to be very plain upon this point, and, happily, this is ' what they have been at Southport. Tata laity have spoken, and the laity will stare.' by the law court* and decline to recognise the Lambeth court as over-riding or watering down the eJclesia.stical law. For the moment :ve need not lay stress upon any other point. Lancashire does not intend to see Protestantism strangled, either for the sake of the Eitablishment, or the pacification of the re- bellious Ritualistic clergy.</p>
        <p>THE emotional nature of S3libiog Patriotism. the Fninch people always</p>
        <p>excuses a good deal of what Would otherwise seem to be extravagance and theatrical display. The welcome given to Major MARCHAND already, will deeply move him, and he may once more sob over F‘shoda and the retire- ment from the Nile. Booming the French Colonial Empire has led to the difficulty, and it ought to be a lesson to us in this country, where the booming of Mr. CECIL RHODES is hardly less realized, and the Jamieson boom wis at one time quite aa extravagant. Tho unreasoning and unreflective mood which France has been indulg- ing in may be taken as due to a curious combina- tion of circunistanceq, which admit of easy state.' ment: But the fervour which st'outed "A Berlin!' now cabs over Fashoda. As Sir W. HARCOURT :marked at Nantyglo, we have to accept our work in Egypt as including what has followed outiide it in tho riN3vely of the Soudan. 1311°</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <p>le at</p>
        <p>there is no reason why we should wc:nt to boom the Soudan as if it were a garden or a fruitful field which would bring us pleasure and profit. We have spent nearly s wen millions of inoney to help Egypt to conquer IVlabdism, and it is not Seven 'trillions we shall sae back in seven years or in seve svien years. To sob over the Soudan and the Ni e, and to shed tears of joy over the commervial out:ook there, is little short of madness. We cannot transform the sand into Verdant fields or acres of golden grain. We can- tot command a decimated population to increase at a rata sufficient to make the destroyed village s and mutilated towns once more flourishing com- munities. The sobs of the sorrowful will not res- tore the thserted laud, nor will the sighs of th faithful make it a land of commerce. Even in dealing with S nith Africa and the Transvaal, we sre not likely to gain anything by our sobs. Most likly PRESIDENT KRUGER Will prove more than a match for Sig A. MILNER at the con- ference, as ho has - already proved his superiority to Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, who imagined he could twist him round his little finger. We are sobbing over the Raid very often. Do we really know all ab •ut it? Does it not seem as if the matters inquired into resembled the extrane- ous things of tile Dreyfus case, compiled to hide the original plot, which probably began in this Country? At least, this is growing to be the general opinion. Is it the opinion of Sir WILLIAM tilatC,UitT, WO wonder. Mr. CEOIL RHODES was at The Durdans yesterday, and conversation on such topics could hardly no avoided. Whatever We may think and feel about the French people and Major MARCHAND, let us not forget that sobbing patriotism Wrii3 current here after the JAMESON raid, and that a lot of young fellows suddenly be- came heroes because it was thought they had gone on a magnificent errand of mercy instead of a fili- bustering expedition. We cannot cornp)te with the Fi emit in excitability or in street displays. They easily beat 11S at these games, and we give up the contest. But we ought to be given to much searching of heart and to be all the stronger for a lesson in political hysterics.</p>
        <p>Memory as to historical events is proverbially short, but conAidering the admiration an Aus- tralian cricketing team justly evokes, and—in ire to the point sti:r--the gate money the it yieids, it seems incredible their reputation has been so quickly built up. A writer in " " has been at the pains to produce some M-ilbourne Press cuttings of a little more than twenty years, and this pr icticallv covers the tale of Australian Prowess. Young Eagland over the seas, in its Wildest imaginings, Lever dreamt that it could equal the prowess of the home country represen- tatives. When their teams won a match they Were told not to be unduly elated, and in all their early vict ries there was a feeling of inferiority, and that the gleam of joy was passing. Now a change has come over things, and although we know that the mother country has no reason to be ashamed of her representatives, yet very few of the counties, teernieg as they are with population, expec6 to vanquish the men of the Southern clime. It is good for Australia that she has shown her Strength in athletic pursuits. It is still better that the strength is recognised with pride here in Great Britain, for the colonies will be more realy to use their reserves for our Imperial advantage. The Botany B4y prtjudice ag linst colonists has van- ished, and the Australian Volunteers, who are now to be seen pasiing in couples through the L nidon streets,areas much a matter of satisfaction to us as our historic buildings and our throngs of people are to them. They are able to report, too, that the compliments which appear in the papers are not idle words, but a- echo of the voice of the nation.</p>
        <p>The dearth of British seamen continues to be looked upon with gravity in the quarters most aftected. Recently the President of the B .ard of Trade met a deputat!on from the N ivy L ,ague, but absolutely declined to bring before the House of Commons a scheine for training lads at the ex- , pease of the Government, at the same time vouchsafing the explanation—not a very gracious one—that if the mercantile burins wanteo B. iti,h seamen they should train them at, their own expense. This is Mr. Ritchie's Way of shelving all awkward problem, ana it will not commend i self to the public any m ire than it did to the Navy League. As a matter of fact the erchmits do not particularly desire British seamen, as they find foreigners equally capable,and willing; to woik at a lower rate ot pay. But British sea- men will be wanted some day—unless the Peace Conference effects even more than its most sanguine supporters expect—and where are they to come from if not Er the mercantile marine ? We agree with Lord Cowles Beresford that it is the duty of the Government to take up these qw.,s- bons and endeavour to find a solution for the difficulty. It is imperative that some meisures shall be adopted for tile fostering of the Britisher'b love of the sea, and the training of the young for life on the ocean wave. We hope the Navy League Will persevere, and not be daunted by the snub from the President of the Board of Trade. Toey Will soon fiod that they have the weight of public Opinion behind them, and that there is no with- standing it we have recent striking examples to Prove.</p>
        <p>Much that seemed mysterious in connection With the recent explosion at St. Halens has been Made clear by the Coroner's searching inquiry. It has wen proved that a spark caused by fried in between two kegs fell upon the vats containing chlorate of potash, and a blaze was the result. But expariineuts have failed to demonstrate at all the explosiveness of chlorate of potash, and in this respect some of the mystery still remains, and must be left to the savants to elucidate. Toe only explanation is that the intense heat genera- ted by the combustion of the chlorate-impregnated vats caused an explosion of the potassic chlorate stored in such large quantities, and the fact that this material will explode under such circum- stances is a chemical discovery nct likely to be lost sight of seeing the tragic manner in which it has been forced home. "Accidental death" was the natural and only verdict possible, and we do laot see that any blame is attached to the United Alkali Company, whose precautions, in the light of the kuowledo of chemical experts anterior to the d.waster,were adequate enough. The recona:nenda- tions of the jury are very sensible, and should, if adopted, as they no doubt will be, tend to lessen the danger of such a catastrophe again taking Place. We deeply deplore the loss of life which 43ccurred, and the p3or fellows who suffored must be looked upon in the light of martyrs to science. Important discoveries in the field of chemistry are !lewdly made at great expense and danger, which la only compensated for ny the lasting benefit they confer on the world at large.</p>
        <p>That odoriferous, multi-coloured stream, S inkey trook, has been breaking bounds and doing con- aiderable damage in the neighbourhood of St. ttulens. On Wednesday night last week it found its way into a disused pit shaft, at the same me tladan.(tring the workings of an adjoining colliery. Ot only that, but by its bad example this evil atrearn tempted the St. Helens and Sankey Canal from its natural course, with the result that, like Other deviators from the straight and narrow Path, the canal ulti,nately found its way to the Pit. Here is a noble opportunity for the moralists. The whole affair is shocking—indescribably shock- ing—but a revelation made later is worse. One Of the correspondents of the daily papers, who is Presumably located at St. Helens,states innocently that "the brook is composed mostly of chemical drainage." This is exactly what several authorities, uutably the Corpira.tion of Warrington, have long triad to prove, only to be met in the chemical quarters with smiling, but flat coutradiutions. here must be something in it—the assertion we</p>
        <p>! mean—for nobody in "Warrington doubts that ; there is a lot of something in the bro.)k. Perhaps both will be cleared up some day. Let us hope so.</p>
        <p>Ihere was certainly" something" in the brook immediately after the recent explosion at S. Helens, and that " something " was vitriol. The contents of several vitriol tanks, containing some thowands of gallons—this was unblushingly ad- mitted at the t me—were turned whhle- sale into the brook. The air surrounding this open sewer was charged with an evil-smelling and nauseating odour which caused considerable discomfort to those living in the vicinity. Not only was the smell abominable but the effect of this cheinically-charged atmos- phere was seen in the 'ehange of colour which it gave to various kinds of ironmongery in the houses. There is this, however, to be said in favour of it that the vitriol may have had the eftect of killing noxious microbes and deodorising the sewage. But still we prefer to be without this St. Helens mixture altogether, and if the St. Helens authorities had heard the remarks which were made by householders who live near the banks of the brook they would have had their sus- captibilities—if they possess any—deeply hurt.</p>
        <p>! Apropos of our remarks recently anent the dis gureinent of the laudscape by the hoardings and lotice-boards of advertisement contractors, we earn that the Landon and South Western Railway Company is about to take a step which will rouse widespread indignation. This enterprising Com- pany has conceived a new method of increasing revenue, which is neither more+ nor less than the ! replacement of the open fences along its lines in the country by hoardings twenty feet high. Such an opportunity for ttie more liberal display of advertisements will no doubt be quickly apprecia- ted by people whose main desire is to force upon one's unwilling attention, the alleged merits of the particular commodity they trade in. Opposi- tion is forthcoming" all along the line "—to bor- row the words of one big "ad "—but it is not at ! all of the orthodox character. One brilliant ! suggestion is that a couple of District Councils should erect on their land a hoarding which would just top the Company's hoarding!by one foot and thus render its advertisements use- less. The idea is that of a genius, but at the same time we ought to remember that the distinction between genius and insanity is not always app tr- ent, and particularly so in this case. Tie adver- tisement contractors and the directors of the ! Company would be foiled in their aim, certainly, but the nuisance and hideous disfigurement would be greater—just twelve inches greater. A better plan will have to be evolved. Perhaps the Company can be persuaded to see reason in another way. Tile R ulway Com- pany's passengers ought to have something to say about the proposal to convert the lines into gloomy tunnels flanked by staring advertisements Ril way travelling at the best of times is not enj ,y- -able, and it would become a trial no one would endure if the journeys had to be made under such conditions as the London and S nith- Western Company's idea involves. We cannot imagine that it will ever materialise. The Com- pany might as well sign its own d-tath- warrant— and the directors too for that matter. There must be a huge number of people thirAting for the blood oE such an unregenerate body already.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>RIINCoRN NuiES.</head>
        <p>RIINCoRN NuiES.</p>
        <p>tBY A CORRESPONDENT ] The Welsh Chapel, RutLial-street, has recently been r Ipaired and renovated, and on Sunday last was formally re-opened. The preacher was the Rev. J.Toomas, d. A., of Liverpool. and large con- gregatioos attended to hear him. The effort on the part oi the clogregation to fqtrry out the altera- tions ha 4 been a nonle self-iacrifi.:ll24 ens; aril it is to be hopui that other Welsh people of t•e town will rally round them in suppott of that effort. As Mr. Thomas puts it: " the ciase is worthy of sup- port if only because it caters for the We!s i allors who visit the port; which was the cause of he touiriation."</p>
        <p>The annual general bleating of the Runcorn Foot- ball Club was one ot con-iderab!e interest, and psrhaps never has a hirer number of members made such 'in rff trt to be present. The chief le 'tars of the wts the election of Pelretaty —over waicn tnere seems ta have bsen a Tne result however, must be very gratifying,to Mr J. Salm (the retiring secret try) wto was elect-A by a mtij )rity of 33 The fikurea were 70 for Ellison and 37 tor Abel. For the good of the club, it is to be booed thar. during the coming saoa tht-e little games k t spii ting htti • a will be abandon:A. Now t.titt election is over, Mr. Elltson may rely on loyal support on all hands.</p>
        <p>Mr. F J. Moulton's trotion v 711,4 to prevent the offi- cials being harrassed by any thoughts of discomfort as the timi cams riund for their Yiere may be reason in it, but there is a good drat more in the fact tit At a good secretary is never harraq•ed. In h,rne.s or out of harness, the competent official will nail his colours to the 111.1Stt and 6 tand or fall by his opinion.</p>
        <p>Feelings of pride fill me ad I recori the nobleneas of the Sprinch Workmen. Runcorn is proud in the possession of such noble spirits. The town is fully aware of the sad mishap over the launching of a large" pontoon " or II ating linling stage, which has been built by a local ship buil ler on the banks of the Manchedter Ship Canal. Two unsuccesstul attempts were made. Then it was that the work- men of the" Sprinch " came forward in a body, and offered their services—gratis—and undertook to launch the " Leviat ian." tqucia then they have been working duriag their spare time, an I have the craft almost ready to go. She is now rigged up, anu presents a creditable appearan .e, so much so, that noon the advent of a favourable tile a sup- ra ne effort will be made, and " she mus-, go." That. workmen should come forward in trio disinterested manner they have, end help an opp3sltion c.mc irn out of a hole like this. is one of the acts that make England what it is.</p>
        <p>Mr E. Gatley roundly a')..l.4ed the Press at the meeting of the Urban District Council, presumably because his remarks up sit certain occasilns do not get reported verbaciom, whilst the remarks of a certain section of councillors gat fully re- corded on all occasions That Was his plea! Whether it is justified or not matters vety little. The duty of a pressman is to record what is of interest to the pub:ie. Thus the conclu- sion must bb obvious ti all. If p sople who love to see their names figure in print woald give u.tsra 4Ce to. those thing. %vetch th-s public have at heart ihAv will certainly figure as (Ate 4as they are heard t) al vantage. Bit-, it &amp;maid be rem-mbered that the Press have to serve the masses ant must have a soul above iadividuslism</p>
