        <p>Arriving at the cemetery the body wag met by the Revs. H. B. 8104g.M. A.., vicar of Frodsham, the Rev. H. G tm, rec ir or M try's, Ch is7,er ; ti l R. E. Eidow-s, 1.&amp;., vicar of El trtford, and the Rw. C. R. Norm, vic tr of Nte (aril Provelcial GranmiCisphslo to the Freemtsoni of Cheshire), with he surp ic'd cLioi? of the church. Tie opnin,g s-eirenee3 of the b +autiful burial servm were u tered by the R3V. El B. B.ogg and as the coffin entored the 3 tete 1 edifice and as it 0/is be- ing convey.ti to 'hi chancel the organist (Mr. Üb.r) tolayei the " D‘ad March" in S tul. After retnneg the 39f i Psil n, the hymn, "N iw the labourer's task ii e'er," wis sung. Toe lesson taken from 15 h chap. 8.. Paul's Epistle to the C )rth. was read by the Row. E. E id° vies. At tho graveside the service was c lode 1 by the ttev,i. El. B. Blom and H. Grantham, ani tne choir man.: "0 God our nelp in ages past." Three vol- leys were fired over the open grave and the most eff.:c- tive ceremony ever witnessed in Frodsiven churchyard condoled. So impressive had the while proceedings ben that numb ens of the large con:ourse assemoie were visibly fir-wed. Wreaths were contribu'ed by :—The Worshipful Master ani Bre' hren of the De Tablev Lodge of Mirk Masons 'No. 396), Frodsham ; the Wtzh.shipful Mister anl brethren ot the Cholin indeley Lodge of /indent Free and Accepted Masons (1938): the officers of the Runcorn D.tactoment of the 2td V.B.C.R. ; Major Smith and officers of the 23d V 8.0 R. ; tho staff of the late Mr A.,13.:.0n's Runcorn office; Nir G Garrett, Fr odsham C irvative Club, t-,e Club Buildings Comp tay, the Coa- servative R g:stratioa Organiz 'don ; Mr and Mrs J iXkleston " with deepee! sympathy at the loss of an old friend ''; the relievin officers of the Runcorn Union, Mr and Mrs W C Burton ; G orge, Amy, Robert, and Frank Ashton, aid G H G matt ; toe "I" (Frodsham) Company of the 2nd V.B C.R, the Rancorn Detechmeet of the 2nl V.B C.R. ; E. Wignill, and E Linak