        <p>Tres Chairman sail he agreed with Mr. unison that they should Act according to the standing orders. There were only five members on the committee to deal with the mat-er, but the whole of tho memeers of the Council were membera of that committee and as he said. twelve months ar, the formation and constitution of commit- tees tendrd to let affairs drift into the ha els of the officials. Would it not he latter to decile that the stind- ing orders of the Council bhould be adhered to, aud any memher finding the stadding orders were not adhered to should point it out. With all one respect to Mr Unisoa ha thought it wAs not necessary to pass the r,..5-lation. Mr. Imieen : I elite agre', with you —With legerd to the Guardians b ieg inconvenienced he understood that the Guardians were satisfied with the answer. Mr. S Taybr thou;ht Mr. Marsaall sh exit' be asked to explain the case. Mr AI ors .all stated that the custom had grown up. that in matt.rs somewhat uoimpertant and matters of detail the committees should deal with them sea rot the Caun .11. He en lerstood the GaArdi ins were eerrectly satisfied with the answer they received. Tne reason the comm t ee came to the conclusion teey had w s be- cause all burial fees ba I to be pAid in a leance and in his-efficiel capacity he was warned tnae if the rule was not strictly adhere I to, it would mean a lose of scores of plan&amp; a year to the Ceased. At the time he saw Mr Ashton and he sail he d nit see why so much shroud he mule of the se=cre s so lone as Mr. Cooke had money in hand to enable rem pay. Mr. Cooke was told that if it wereeinconvenie ttm pay the fees on application he could pay them the same day any time befere five o'eluck Tee letter to the ri•lAr - wad ceurteoasly wordei. T leave the pasmeets over • for a week weuli disorganise t'oeir fi Aeneas and p -event them closing tlrtir accounts as sharplyj as they ought to b.. closed. The Chairman said as the resolution was not second -d the matter ended. It