        <p>Mr. Brighonse, coroner, neiTi an inquest at the Police Parade R) .m on Monday on the body of James Charles Youn: who received fatal injuries whilft cycling on tin previous Thu rs lay. Feederick Y u ig, residing at Fearnheari, said the de- ce=ise,l was his brother and was 26 years of age and lived at 8 Jli i-ereet. He was a master paiater. Witness saw ha b other preyi els to him go:ng to Golborne on Vim Tnursd4y but ha I not seen him since alive. Hu knew nothing about Os aceidont. Coarles Higginbit'om, 13, Queen- tree", said he left Ws rritiron on Tnurs,lay afternoon, the 25th inFt , with Yrung They start..ed about 2 o'clock to go to Golborne, abut 7 mEes distance. They had gone there on buii- Lesi, and c. m nenned their return journey about a quarter to Moe. Tnev mounted jest outside G 'thorn() as there was a nasty brow. Deceased went ab mm 40 Tards An I the machine slipped, aril he fell on his sh eilder :—Br the Coroner: Yee, the machine had " akidded."—Continuing, witness said he we tup to deceased and rased lYm, and found he sya, bl :elm; at the nose. Deceased kept saying" I'm bad." He could riot stand him -If. As' inng man came to witness's help and de.ceas(-1 wan taken home in a w,,ggonetre, arriving about 11. o'clock at night.—By the C ironer : He was sober a 11-)ri they started from G Ile had had some:drink but only "three or four".—Tho Coroner: It if no Ilie blam ng 'he cycle or anything else,and y or say he was sober ? —Witnese : Yes, he was sobir It was a tine clay ?—Yes.—Was he an exper;- erseed rider ? Yl.e, he was a good rides— How did the medical man know he was not very sob r at tbe time? Toe doctor asked me if be had bad any- thing to drink, and I said yes: Dr. Jam's 8 Sheri. it; Id-street, said he was c,lled in to see the deceJsecl on Fr d iv. The decsace i was a very s'eady man and hid worked for him a bit. He was eseledi 'or briizht and enerzetic. A message came for tim on Thurseay to come down, but no name or adAr.ess was left, rod wien the second message came he went at ence. Thar, acconnted for the lateness in SOPIIII, him. D)cealed died v 10-45 p.m the ,ame day. Witness Lit sure he would di., and toll d-cea•4ed so.</p>
        <p>The Coroner then read to the jury a Irtter from Dr. Billupps, which stat.d that deco %sad was In a c )matose condition when he saw him. He believed the cause of Oetth was due to the fall producing concussion of the brain from a tracru-e of tie skull. There were symp- toms cf trac-ure at te has-) of the skull. N.:r 44 Hind E ,z•b th Pacocks said Dr. Sherratt asked her to go and see deceas-d at his house. She was present at his death on Friday night. The Coroner explaiuod that deceased's wife was un- able to be present, as she herself was under medical care.</p>
