        <p>We understand th it 04 sa-- ice,'.ajor Sir Francis. Synge. Baer., of tle Ist ,B c alien ttle Peince of Wales's Vieueteers, $O-i(th- Lancaseire Regiment (40th Foo- ) at Pees on, attained t .e age of 48, and will con,ecriently have ' to: l: Ave the army on pension, havine reacher the age limit. Tile gallant baronet join d the service soon °Ler he attained his majority in 1872, and he was promoted in the fol- lowing yeir. tie re ,c led company rank in July, 1883, and fi .Id tank ten ye .vs tater. For five years Sir femme. 1141 the eeja aricy of the 4 4 Battalion the King's Livert.ooi Regiment, (the old .2nd Royal Lancashire Militia) at Warrington, and he eas also served at Warring on in command of the South Laneashire Depts., Colipani a. CYCL 'S.—Special Ladies anti Gents Machines at .£7 15s See Windows J e 3. GARLAND. Brid4,-st.. Warrinzton. LIOINS 13 i4T GLaolki BOOPs. Asa, SU 155 UNDER Tull CLOCK RRIDGIC-STRRH T.—Advt. (300 D TRIIPLAILY AT WARRINGTON. •;: 1 The usual vv,:ekiv ne:.s.on of Ve "Ou ward " Lodge of the 1.0.G.T was hel I 'on . Tuesday in the old County Ceurt, Bank—t 0,4. Bro.fi. Wright presi- ded. After the usual Lodge business .had been transleted, Bro. the Rev J. A. Lee, D.O.T. (Liver- pool) dalivere I an addieki, urgiog the members to more active work, and adming• (hem to hold half- yearly conforenoSs. Short arldreeses were delivered by Bros. O'Brien, Sparks and J Sheath, congratulat- ing tee LOCige on being the means of instituting a new Lodge in Werringtoe. Bros. Conolley and Litherlend replied. thanking the speakers for their kind remarks on bell 11l of the " Star of the East" Lodge. A vote of thanks was tenlered to Bro. Lee for his presence and exceilent address. The Lodge was closed in due form by Bro. . Wright, C.T.-- On Wednesday evening the weekly meeting of the I" T -mperance Mission " Lodge Was held in the Academy stre4 Mission liooln. Bro. O'Brien pre- sided over a fair attendance. One candidate was admitted Into the "Leder by initiation. Bro. Roberts, L.D., real the digest of business for the No. 1 Convention Ses.iee to be held at Sutton, en IJune 7th. Bro. J. Sneath gave an excellent report Hot the Dietriet iiodge session at Seuthport lag Sattit day. The rep,. t was aeopt ed. Bro. J. Sheath , reported having visited the Wernngton 'Onward" 1 bodge on the previous evening. Bro. O'Brien also spoke. The Lodge was Closed in due form by Bro. O'Brien, C.T , pro tern uNoßit Tag ULO.J.K. FOR GCOD ROM —Advt. Have tett seen the Nliqf Poitic stroe that Z. 13.1YLEY IsAac hss opened in Ho-sernarket-str-,et.—Advt. THE WARRINGTON SAILING CLUB. The seeoud nett of this sea* n. t esk place on Saterday afternoon, over the club coulee at Rowley Q. as.y. The distance was three Utilise round. The following boats turned our, viz :—Saint, Messrs. Strinaer end Whittle; Penitent, Me,srs. Jefferson; and Mats, Messrs. Cnappull and Garnett, for prizes, preseo by the Captain timi the Clu'o The act- ing efti tt.r was the Captain, Air. l homes Whittle, woo despef cued the boats at 335. in a nice whole- sail bre. ze from she north-west. Tee Stint was the fint over, closely ((Mowed by Male, with Penitent some seen is lab,er. The b .ats ran all in a cluster to the Milner Lock, whore it was anise of haul sheet for the beat to the top mirk. The Penitent and Mali here draped the Saint, which was going to windward very badly. Thi P. nitent was first to bear round the top mark,with the Male on her heels and with the wind aft. the pair BOOR made short work of the top reach. The Saint had by this time got round the top mark, and picked up consi- derably on tee leaders in the run, they " gybing " round the mark for the first almost tegether. On the run up for the second round, Mali g3t by Peni- tent, and the pair bud a keen race for the top mark, the Penitent just managing to fetch round it first. this Maia having r i m tee ano:her board. Running for the finish of the second round, when the boats came en tbe wind, Main, got by the Penitent and " gybel " roand the mark with the leed,Saint again pinking up on the run down. In the short cross tacking for the top mark Penitent got by Main and rounded first. No alteration of the positions took place after this, and the Penitent eroseel the line winner of the Captain's prize by 36 seconds froin the Main, the Saint crossing some minutes later. The ofileial„times. were as under, viz:—</p>
