        <p>tetters. to the T-ZAttor.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESFONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Authnts :erteri •nrended for pu,ltion must in al ewe send their raiveg and addre to the E Ma-t, r• I, t“rd d or pubication must b 3 writttn on one side only of the p +per. Correvondents are wrongly advised to write briefly. A shorz V :ter has much greater chance of insertion than a long one.</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CfIURCII CHOIR. Sir,—lt is not m 5 custom to reply to anonymous letters, but I feel bound,iii d .fonce of a most caplble and paiw- takine organist, to briefly state that the Vicar,Wardens, ancl all thi s- of tie c ,ogregatioo to whom I have Spoken on Ihe sut,j.er. since your last isup, are quite sati.fled with Mr. Viriik,is ability, tict and enthusiastic work sq orgaoist an-I choirmaster of S. —Yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>ERNEiT R. T&amp;RBUCK.</p>
        <p>24-h. 1899. [The ab ye letter is in rPply to one which appearei in this colturn lAst week, signed " Sidesman" 'ro'n enquiries which we h ,ye made ti.is week, we learn that" Sidesmin" is not a si .BrllFirl at it Paul's Church, and we Feel bound in instice to the ex'sting sidesmen to make this fac known Soma, ExAmjsEß '</p>
        <p>Sir,—Kindly allow me a few lines in reply to letter on the ab .ve subject in pair iasue of last Friday week. I may saY that it shows very bad taste, and want of respect fu. the church services, to bring soch matters into the public 0A pns unless all other means have failcd. If "S d " had any c mplaint to make, a b tter couree would have been to speak to the orgahist, and then, if need he, to th, vicar. A v,luntary choir in a gilar!e of worship cbes not expect to receive attacks from people as ignorant of music as" siciesman " appe frs to i be, especially if that cooir s as able as the one at St. sin a Dissenting organist and choirmaster in this tovro, and managed to leave my duties at Chrit tmas to bear St. Paul's choir render the greater part of Handel's .6 MehB`ah " under the conduc, ordhip of Mr. Wilk s, the organist. and I was surprised and delighted to find a choir an carefully trained that the boys were able to sing the difficult music of that oratorio th r ughly well. - When leaving the parish, It:!v. H. Y. Pigot, himself no !mean authority on musical matters, stated that he was leaving the music of the church in quite as satisfactcry a state ai he found it, which was saying a ereat deal. Up to the present, Mr. Wilkes has seemed to be in the nalortona-e positiin of having the good word ef every- one, an,. I, though in some matters a rival of Mr. Wilkes, feel the greatest prr-onal respec" and admiration for ot e who hai flown such conspicuous ability as a musician, as to be able to ke .p the music of an imoortant church like S. Paul's at such a high standard. Thanking you in anticipation, I temain, NONCONFORMIST ORGANIST AND CHOIRMASTER.</p>
        <p>"HONOUR TO WHOM HONOUR IS DUE." "Sir—Yu published last Fr day, a continual !ated report cf an interview with the YM C.A S, cretarV, which contained a statement to this effect "that alien the pteient secretary was appointed no spiritual work was dose during the week" and be then goes on te say bow he c, mmenced a Bible class, Now, I think there is some mistake here, for, to my knowledge, Mr. Wylie bad conducted a Bible class on Friday nights which was mach enj yed by the members, for a long time previous to the present Secretary's advent.—Yours respectfully,</p>
        <p>AN ONLOOKER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>SCIENCE AND ART- PRIZE DISTRIBU-</head>
        <p>SCIENCE AND ART- PRIZE DISTRIBU-</p>
        <p>TION AT EA RLES I OWN.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening, the prizes zainel by the stu- dents attending the science, art, and commercial classes field at the Vaduct Institute in connection with the Newtkn-in-Makerfield Technical Instruction Committee, were presented by• Mr. W. Thompson, jun., a member of the committee. There were piesont on the platform Messrs. G. W. Emmett, T. Ormand, P. Connor, W. Davies, W. G. Smalley, B. Davies, and T. R. Bradley, and besides the stuilehts, a large number of their friends and parents attended. Mr. Thompson before praceeding with the distribution expressed the pleasure it gave him to attend there that night for the purpose of presenting the very handsome prase they raw before them to the students who had been successful at the</p>
        <p>Institute classes. He thanked his fellow committee num for the honour they bad done him in asking him to p ',- lona that euty. He felt at the time that a much be t.r selection might have been made, as he did not claim any special qualification for the duty, but he did claim to recognise the importance a,cl absolute necessity of technical education. From a general survey of the report for session 1898 he eoesidered the re- Sults had been satisfac- ory, although there were many items in dt tail which the Committee desired to see improved in another session. He commented upon the reduction of the Technical Instruction grant from the County Ceuacil, and said this no doubt Recount si for the di crease in the num,er of students in 1897 and 1.89 d as the Committee ha I felt compelled to curt expeni es by discontinuing classes in Laundry and Ccelrery. The classes at Wargrave had also to be suspended owing to an insufficient number of students attending toe classes to justify the Committee in carrying on the claes(s. After referring to the comparative statement of results be said it was urich to be regretted that the Gold Mecal offer dby Dr. Handyside had not been won this year. The highest number of marks gained by any student was 8, tut 9 marks was the min,inura. Ho hoped next year to s e a hard race for this m, dal. Last year it wee won by A Hill with an aggregate number of marks of 13 He was pleased to congratulate those stu lents who had gained prizes, which he knew was the result of close application and a great deal of v ry hard work. The praes, although hindsome, viiluab'e and useful were of little worth compared with the value of the knowledge the students hal acquired. (Hear. bear.) Be impressed upon those who had not gained prizes the desirability of continuing their studie4, and not to be dis- couraged. The size list was a handsome one and the school was fertunate in possessing such a fond. The amount subscribed for that distribution was £4B 18s, wisich was exactly the same sum as was subscribed for the last dist ihution. This was by far the largest ertrunt subscribed in any similar district. He knew a neighbouring district, double the size of Earlestown, with double the population and double the number of Students, where the total of the prize fund vest only about £3O He afterwards dwelt at some length en the question of continu tion classes pointing out the advant- ages which woul i aceriist to students atten ling these classes; Continuation classes had been aptly cl,scribed as the golden bridge connecting the Technical with the eleinrwary schools. He afterwards proceeded with the distribution of the priz.a, the follnwing blies., the ti t of successful studeets with the number of ensiles gaieed.— Bailey John, 1 ; Paley Peter, ; 13ihiff William, 24 ; Ewell% }Velar I 1; aei'bv Waiter 11, 5; Bradshaw fl-r- -bert, ; Brodie Kite, 3; Bullock James A, 8 ; Bunting Georce, 44; Buetin,^ Mary T, ; Burgess James. 2 ; Chisholm Gre,trude, 1 ; Clough Thomas E, 1; Co;kley Selina. A, i; C,ok Tom, 4; Cruickshank Helen. 1; Davies Mary A, i; Davies Wenleck, 1 ; Day Joseph, 24; Deady Arthur J, 24; Dring Samuel, ; Drucninond .1 ihn, 2 ; Drurnrened William, 24 ; Eaton Arthur, 24 ; E,tketsley John F, 24; Edington Harry. 8; Eeierton Frei k. 2; Fin. nine Shirley M, 4; Gannon Fredei ick. 4; Gee To, mis H. 34; Gilmour J, ho 4; Graham Aenie, 1; Gr Jahn, 1; Green Jeseoh, 1 ; Grimshaw Ether, ; Grimshaw Samuel, ; Harrison George A, 1; HArvey Lewis, 4; Baynes Cissar dra L. 4; Hill Albert R. 14; Hedkin.,-n Joseph, ; Huline Reward G. 4; Hutchings Fred. ; Jankseo Ge dtge E, ; Jackson William ; Joie s .Alfred. 1 ; Jones Ernest, 2 ; Jones John, 4; Kidd Bar-id P. 2; lieow,es Gertrude, 2; Lathern John 11, : Leith ,r Male,l E, 4; Linton Ciement, 1 iveraeri El.z •beth, 1; Lonew‘ r: h Marv, 2; Lynne Albert, 34; Deeffaese Colin, 14; Mikehern Emma, 14; MArehein Hater,24; Msrrow Bea ; Ms' h-r J imes. 2; Icy Thomas, 3; 'Mayor Har,bl P, ; Monks Waiter 3, 34;