      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>ST. HELENS EXPLOSION AND ITS CAUSE. THE CORONER'S INQUIRY.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS EXPLOSION AND ITS CAUSE. THE CORONER'S INQUIRY.</p>
        <p>VERDICT OF ACCIDONTAL MATH.</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall, St. Helens, on Tuesday, Mr. S I Brighouse, coroner, resumed the inquest upon the bodies of the five men who were kul4 on the 12th Inst., by the explosion at the Kurtz works of the United Alkali Company, Limited, Sr. Hel-ns. The names of the deceased were Frederick Faram, 61, Argyle street; JAn Grey. 35, Manor-street, Peas- ley Cro-s ; Richard Swift, 23, Park road ; Richard Gibs )a, 5, Frazer's-court ; nod Thomas Duffy, 42, Bariesr- ttreet. The Coroner was accompanied by Colonel Ford, Her Majesty's chief inspector of explosives, and Mr. H. Richmon I, superintendent inspector of workshops and factories. Mr. E. Stewart Brown appear- d for the relatives of Swift an the executure of William Boyle, who own pro- perty in the neighbourh ; Mr. J. C. Eccles repre- sented Mrs. Faram, and Mr. Riley the widows of Duff' and Gibson. The United Alkali Company were represented by Mr. Horn i Ige, barrister-at-law, and there were also present Sir David Gamble, and other directors of the Company. The first witaess examined was John Woods, loco- motive d iver. He stated that on the morning of the 12th he was engaged in -onveying some empty chlorate f potash le,,gs to the doors of toe chlorate-house, as here they were to be refilled. While delivering the kegs he heard a sho tof "Fire," and seeing a flame in time cool- ing chamber of the calorate-bouse he ran away. About nine minutes afterwards there was an explosion. Tne witne a swore that no spark went from h;s engine into the cooling chamber. Bate, apprentice cooper, who was receiving the kegs from the last witness, explained that as they were h ,nded to him he placed them on the gr eine and relied them through the door of the cooling chamoer and berween the two rows of cooling vats. He had two k ga qu to close to the doorway on their bilges, and then received another one. Instead of putting this tnird keg on its bilge, he rested it on end on the floor and against one of the other two kegs. Its weight caused the others to run forward, and as the eiges scraped to- gether a spark flew out and fell upon one of the vats. This was followed immediately by a bl..ze which ran right along the vats. Witness tried to turn on water from the hose, but failed to do so; he then ran off and give the alarm. The rxolosion took place from five to t in minutes later.--Cross-examined, the witness said he did not run away because he feared an explosion, but because the beat drove him wcay.—ln answer to Colonel Ford, she witness said he never saw a spark come from the kegs befo-e in this way. Alfred Budge, manager of the works, was the next witness. He was fir-t questioned as 'o the wa'er Sup- ply. The Company, he explained, had their own reser- voir and pumping statim, the latter le-ring half-way be- tween the rsservoir and the chlorate house. The pipe lines from the reservoir passed through a brick field, from which the clay had 10-em t Iran off, and this inter- ferine, with the water pressure, a fresh line of pi r es had been recently laid between the reservoir and the pump- ing station. At the time of the outbreak of fite these pipes were being tested by water pressure, and the con- sequence was that the pressure up-in the mains between the pumping station and the chlorate house was less than usual. This caused some minutes' delay in getting the water on. When the fire broke out, continued Mr. Rudge, he was examining these pip% and was 700 yards away from the chlurate-bouse Noticing a small clou I of smoke in its neighhourtio-d ke went toward the hu,leieg, and the explosion took place before he got therm. There had never been an explosion at the works tef,re, and chlorate of potash had not been regarded in itself as an explosive,. About feur er five veers ago a yowls who was using a chisel in the millnom produced&amp; spark and caused a fire, but this was extingeished. A^ the time of the accident the cooling-room contained 54 tanks, which were filled with a solution ef chlorate of potash in water. Each tank held 6 cwt. of chlorate in crystals. Tney were wooden tanks, lined inside with lead. In the stnrernom adjoining there were two heaps of kegs each keg heeding 1 cwt. of chlorate. Ole heap would contain 96 tons and the other ab int 58 t ms, or 154 tons in all. Of these, 15 tons were powdered chlor- ate, and the remainder was in the form of crystals, the Ty-me, rd chlorate being close to the wall whieh divided Ithe storeroom from the cooling-room. The chlorate was I brought to the refinery in an unrefined elude ion from other parts of the works. In the cooling-room there wen two sets of cooling tanks, one set of iron and the other of wood lined with lead. The chlorate was put into these tanks will water, and afterwards the water was drawn off and the crystals taken out. the crystals were ether put into kegs and stored, or were sent into the millroom to be ground as powder. In answer to the C moner, the witness said neither the powder nor the cryetals would ignite alone. If you had this powder on the floor, and somebody went in and dropped a lighted match on it, what would happen P—Practically nothing. There would be a splutter from the match until it was consumed. How do you account for this instantaneous fire in the cooling-room ?—Of course anything saturated with the chlorate—like a man's clothes for instance—would burn with intense rapidity. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>How do you accougt for the fire spreading so rapidly? It is inexplicable. The other fire did not +Tread so rapidly. I almit that there is chlorate of p itash on the wood tar.lu. It is unavoidable, and you cannot be surprised at the speed with which these things would ignite. Then if there is wood imorlgnated with chlorate of potash, it would burn quickly if a light were applied?— Oh vs.</p>
        <p>Were the wooden keg. in the storerolm also impreg- nated P—No. The powder is absolutely dry, and it is only when is is damp or wbt that the chlorate gets into the wood.</p>
        <p>Amked why such a large quantity of chlorate should base been stlred undar the same roof as the c,oling room, the millrolm, and the drvinvrown, tie witness repaatel that hithetto the chlorate had been considered to bs free from explosive properties, and the pla^e had theref re been thought to be safe. He admitted that if these keys of chlorate bad been stored elsewhere there w .uld n. obablv have been no explosion.</p>
        <p>_ By Mr. Horridge Chlorate would not burn alone, hnt if it were brought in contact with carbon in man's clothes it was infl mnaable, though not explosive. In grower to q leattous by Mr Richmond, the witness agreed that it would be safer to have the vats of iron in furure, and also to store the chlorate further away from the rooms in which the various processes were carried on. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Richmond: I should like to get your idaa as to how the explosion occurred. I suppose it might occur either the 'ugh the mir.ure of the carbon contained in the wooeen kegs wi.h the ehlerate or through the escape of the gas given off at a certain heat by the chlorate and the carbon givan off by toe woo ?—ln the first instance I think, it has be-n caused by the evolut'on of the oxp,en gas, due to the high temperature, and by the canon from the burning wood. Ths carbon and the oxygen together would cause the exnl, sion ?—Yes ; but we have not been able to pro- duce such an explosion again by a small experiment. The Coroner : If these conliog tanks,inEead of being made of lead and wood, had been made of iron, and a fire h4d taken plac in the way described, would there , have been any mischi•if at all P—Probably not anything I like what occurred.</p>
        <p>Woe, wou!cl there have been to take fire 2—Only the three wooden kegs. Further qtreationei by Mr. Richmond, the witness said the kegs in qurstion cacao from Messrs. Bryant and Msy, to be refiVed with chlorate ot potash. He did not kn-otv that there was any phosphords a iherilg •o the Irek.s. At the conclusion of his examination, Mr. Rtrtge was complimented by Om Coroner upon toestraialvtor- w .rd ma lner in ;chief.' he had given his evidence Dr. J. J. Bert, manager at the Hardphaw Brook Works which adj .in the K .rtz Works and r owned hy the same Company, next described the sxolnsicn. He said that, seeiog large volumes of white 8M kl iFsuing fro n the direction of the chlorate-house, he made his way thither. He found it impossible to ent.r the Place, on a 'count of the fiance which were runnini, from one v..t .0 another. Sc ‘ing rh it the buil tint might not be saved, he ordered the men who were tht r to cer away out of dander. S tme of them sug ,ested the a loisa,hility of trying to save the adj t'niog sawmill and t ,er build- ard accordingly be t.ent them to the Harrishaw Brook vve,,,, for additional hosepipes. In the mean- tim. the fire had burned through the rlof of the cooling chamber ard was forcing its way to the Et ,T ern( m roof wi'ness looked inro the storeroom and found it full e! den e white smoke. Soon the roof and the top- most keys caught fire, and th , heat was so forenan that he had to leave the door and retreat about 25 yards. Wrier' the fief., got down to with;n three feet of the ground it increased at a terrific rate, awl he ring the rear of som-thing coming, witness an I those with him ran towards the brook. When they had got forty yards away they turned, and saw the rxplrsion take place. Wi nesa WaS thrown to the ground. His impression at first was that a baer had exploded. He had an idea that the chlorate would explode, •s was proved by the fact that he had looked into the storeroom within a minute of the explosion taking place. By the Coroner : Hitherto the wooden vats had been used at many works, but there cou'd be no doubt that iron ones were eater. Be did not consider it dangerous to rtll empty kegs feceen the two tows, f c oing tai.ks. Aft.r this exp rience, he admit-.ed ti a' it would be het, er not ta et ire 6hl..trate at the chloratr-housts— , no danger wis anticipated from cloing so. Unioubtedly. if the chlorate had not been in the store- ro m there would not have been an explosion, but only a fire. _</p>
        <p>.• • ,• I reply tn Mr. 1101.14 v, witnass said he Vinught the hra, mus-. have been 1,700 d (Ce,itiva-le). This intense heat, coupled h the preqPnce of such a 14rge Quantity as 154 tor s ot chlorate, woul 1, he thought, cause an ex ..losion. -</p>
        <p>Two working coopers having given evidence, John Lucia, manager of the central ccoperage at the works, stated that he never issued instructions to h;s men to</p>
        <p>particularly examine the kegs from Messrs. Bryant and May for traees of ph sehoreus. Teey were simply told to " see tn.it the Ite!s were p f c'ea 1, well coop red, and fit f r re-pall-dee." In reply to C donel Ford, till witness Fa :1 no hai never kno .vri any Oni- pliorcus to be foiled on casks retuined by M esrs. Bi van,, and May. Toorws Wileon S:ewart, technieal menager of the works, aI stited, Hi answer to Colo-lel F.r I. th t he had never known any of Messrs. Bryant and May's casks to cen'ain tracls ef phosphorus or match Colll,l3iia3Cl on being returned. Mr. Brown: flas the idea of such a thing as phos- phorus adhering to these c4slee been considered or con- templated before this inquiry 2—Not to my know- ledee. This was all the evidence offend. The Ceroner, in sninmine up, pointed orc to the jery that their duty was simply to find out the cause of death, and to say whether any person was e,Lnioally resp-inkibl ; the qeestion of civil negligence and as to wheteer the owners of the works should pay compnnsation to the re• latives was qiitc outside their jurisdict on. B sides the question of the exoleftion, he went on, there was al- ways the danger of firs at these works. He was fre- quently holding inq PiSt4 upon persone who had been burned on account of their (lathing, which was impreg- nated wi7h chlorate of pota.h, being set on fire. I: might be true that the chlorate by itself would not take fire, but when it became impregnated with something else, either with in m's clothing or the wood surrounding the cool'n vats, then there was a dan ler of fire. sAemed to him that if this danger of fire always existed the various prrzessee eh iuld be carried on under coali- tions which in case of. a fire would render the outbreak al harmless as possib a. In the present case it was eke,- that if there had not b .en this large qaaltity of chl rat stored in a room adjoining the sceee of tie fire th ere would have been no explosion, and probably no danger to lif 4. It was also admirte I that if the cooling vats or tanks had been nil&amp; of iron there woe' i have ben no danger, even though the three kegs had caught fire, the floor of the room being of cement. But nobody seemed to have thought that chlorve of potash would explo le under any circumstances, and this was the reason why such a large amount of the chlorate was upon the pre- mises.</p>
        <p>The jury found a verdict of " Accidental death," and made the following rec-eninenclathns :—(1) That all buil logs of the chlorate plant shaulti be fireproof; (2) that all packages should be fireproof; (3) that the cool- ing tanke should be made of iron inste.d of svoo,l ; (4) that better precautions should be taken for dealing wito outbresks of fire; (5) that the amiunt of the stork i any building should be limited by Grrernment control, and such building shi,ild be firepno and isolated from the process of manufacture.</p>
      </div>
