        <p>FATAL CA.RELES,..yN r.SS AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ROME wORK3 FOREMAN KILLED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Coroner Brigoous • held tne io.q ret,t at the Griffin Inn, Peasley Cress*. Helens,on Saturda7, on the body ot Thomas Smart, age 4 sixty-ece, of 1, Ex 'ter- troet, a foreman at Messrs. Cannington and ohaw' s Glisibottle Works, who died tu tie S. Helens Ho,pital on We i nes- day night, shortly atter being run over by a couple of railway wageos,. rt.ar the works. Mr. Bland, fr, in the Lime-street Offices, and Inspector Whistle 'nit wet-, present on behslf of the London and North-Wt at rn Ratmay Company. Mrs. Smart spoke to her husband dying in the hospital, he having hid one :eg almost cut off and a e3mpounel fracture of the other thigh. .</p>
        <p>Jelin L we, of 21, R .bins-lane, a railway shunter, said that about a quarter to five on Wednesday afternoon he was engaged with a locom-)tive shunting on the Raver.h al branch. They had seen seven warns. Toe waggoris and ent.,itie were coming down the incline all on the move. Wnea the end waggon got near the hand gates leading from the towing-path of the canal he unhooked it. Just at that moment deceased, who had coins along the sin) of toe canal for the purpose of g )ing to work, appeared on tne (Nur side of the waggon. He shunted to him to keep back, but the deceas-d popped in bttween the one waggon and the other six, and the six waggons knocked him down and ran over him. By Mr. Bland : After the acciient the deceased was conscious, and said to him that it was his own fault. James Welsh, bottle-blower, of 74, Wilson-street, said that on Wednecday he went t ) work along with the de- ceased. It was raining heavily, and he deceased gave him a shelter under his umbrella. When they got to the ga,es on to the railway decea7ei went first. Witness followed, and was just insida the gate when deceased was knocked down. It was no one's fault but de- cea9ed's hr walking between the waggons when they were movi )g. The Coroner said they could not wonder at these things happening if people walked between waggons in that way. Toe jury returned a verdict of " Accidental deatb,', exprehaing the opinion that the accident happened owin to deceased's own carelessness.</p>
      </div>
