      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>NOTES Og G4=4,3i AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES Og G4=4,3i AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>Warrington experienced the bitterness of adversity 911 Saturday in their game with Liverpool. Th 9 speiltators turned up in unusually strong numbers o watch the game. Dr. Smith sfl Thewlis opens It e b Acing and the former, be fore he bad opened his account,was giv,m out lbw. Fl A Denham succeeded h r. hi stay was very brief. lhe um:ord of the other perfo•mers was some- what similar and would be but a repetitt in. Let the b U statert •nt suffice that the home te int were dis- nrssed for 36 Tnew display was an excellent one, •nd, tAting into consideration the in in( eff•ct of the ex hi hi ion by the other batsm-n, was a fine performsnce— he carried his bat out for 23 Giedwia's prior name 4itu the hall was a insritlrioui one and pans out at eizint wick ts for ten runs.</p>
        <p>That the wicket was nnt to blame is proved by the vigitora' perf ,rnsanC.. Rune cgme steadily, Warring- . . .</p>
        <p>ton's score berng soon overhauled. The bowling was liirly well 6h Ired but there was no escape f -a. Warring- ton and the innings clos4d with a m way for the vie t )rs of 136. There were some capi at performances inciuded ia tale t )tal. ME A-hcroit hails the last wth 54. W N Barlow was credit-id 'vita 30 runs and R es'ewiit Brown compiled 25. The Rev El E Stanley made 22 not out so that %arrington were fairly out- classed. The wicket was not to blame for this so there was only one: wis nit wait it should have been. Our premier team are a wry erratic eleven: One week their play is all cue could wish and at other Lilies it 11 j sit is d.44pJoi tier. However we must take things as they come and hope for toe best.</p>
        <p>The second elevens of Warringten and Liverpnol played at Aigburth but the result in this cue was mere iavourable for the Warrin.Rton men. The Liverpullians by steady Way cnrepluid a score of 99. Roper, D !rbyshire, and ltiAlls.dtte were prominent amongst the tatters. The innings qf the Warrington men was in tome respects a farce. Leaving out C. E. C. Kandle's score. only 19 runs were got by the ethers. The player men,ilned, Rave a brilliant exhbbiti-n at the wicitot. From K.ndlt.'s hat came a five, 11 fours, and 4 threes, which is good evidence of strong hitting pow. -s. and the flint that his sc9re was only three short of the century, entitles him to commiseration. The new player should prove an arq risition to Warrington.</p>
        <p>The Sankey Wire Mills, who seqm to be getting into their strode, have most con:lusiviily beaten High Legh. The game was played OD the ground of the latter on Saturi'av and Cooko f r th , visitors credited hirn-ielf wi at 30 not oir. Wo lo •Ic and Pritlnard als/ batted to cod ouronFe making 27 and 20. The home eleven totalled 63. l3rztindale and Hamden registering 30 and 11 respeclvely.</p>
        <p>The result of a match 13,tween St. James's And the Post Offi:m on Saturday W4S a win for the Stints by 5 runs. Cox bitted well for the Poen Office and Pitrick's innings for the winling tin real s d 12. Bite's trundling for tho winners vt49 good, he taking 5 wickets for 9 runs.</p>
        <p>Hough Green sufPrrdd ,feat at the hanis of an eleven from Messrs. Pete r Walk Ar and S-e* on Saturday. The brewery men compPed a rore of 72, of which R Cookl m 30,4,11 tip, voliter (U .oh Green) were despatched for 32. J Ralliwell batted well for the vbitors hls innings reAlisin,e; 20.</p>
        <p>The ChAshire Lin,a Engineers inftieted defeat on S. Helens St. Thomas eleven by 38 runs. Haekitt played well for the Enginers whi ev;dently 1:6-mes3 good bowlers in the persons of J. Monks and Duckwcirth.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall St. Wilfrid's secured the victory in their h S ,ntov W.tileyanc, at Lnv •Iy-lane, nsat;ur- day hr a gond maj ritv. The latter are not playing up tither ti;ual form, or tis would not have happened.</p>
