      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>WARRINGT3N C.TIA.MBER OF COMMERCE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGT3N C.TIA.MBER OF COMMERCE.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Conncil of tat) irriugton Chamber of Commerce was held at Market-gate Chambers, on Monday, Mr. Robert Garnett in the chair. The secretary (Councillor Bennett) sub- mitted the ranort of the delegater to the 39 u annual meeting of the Associated Chambers of t'ommerce, held at the lintel M.etroi.ble, liendon, on the 14th, 15-h, and 16th March, 1899,— Mr. Beanett also stated that he attended the annual meeting of the secretaries, on the evening of the 14-h, and was again much intereeeed in comparing not es with the secretaries tif the various other chit. mbers. As he pointed nut last year, they were nearly all numerically very much stronger than we in Warringten. in several instances, the whole holy of members were summoned several times a year. and in 801700 cases reporters were admitted to every meeting. Huildcr-field had 400 me ,bers, Southampton tkatly 300, Swansea over 200, and Walsall not 1.-ss than 150, whilst little Luton blast‘ o f a membership of f rur to every 1,000 of the pepu'at ion, our own proportion being only abut one t 3 every 1,500. The generality of the chambers represented seemed to aim at greit- er comprehensivenems than we,and welcomed every- body who wa.t interested in any phased local com- merce ; whilst the impression left upon his mind as the result of various conversations with the a cre- taries whom he met was to the effect that the utility of our local chamber might be much increased if more of our leading commerial men would take an active an 1 continneus intere4t- in its affairs Cellec• i rely the Chamb,Tt af Commerte of the Ueited Ifinadom were a genuine commercial parlia- ment, aid exerted enorineus inflaenci noon the Gevernment in every department of cenam.ircial activity. Individurally each Chamber might be- come the very mainspring of the commerce of its own immediate locthty."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-02_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>THE cIUICIDE OF A YOUNG HAIRDRE3qER. MEWCA LING I'dE ft ISE' AOC FJR I.'WO YE %JIB. Mr. J. C. Bate, c .raner he lai inq i ot, on F:1.1 ay afterna•n, it the P ii:c S aaon, Fr yisriam, on the body of W:1 er Brart, aged 19, who cemmitted sui :We by join jig iat tee Weav r Canal ne ir Bialee's Lock. on vi..des lay eveniag. Mc. J .mes Elavards was foreman of tile jury. Fra, cis Bratt, father of the deceased,said his son W. 1,4 Tired by Dame Lip•rett, hairdrieeer, Aooey. street, Accrington- He received is. 61. per week, with board 1 ideing anl washing. Twa years ago deceased Was empllyed at a hairar.3,er's in Runcorn, during whice time he dyed wita his sister. On one occasi in his sit 'r saw him take something to the elosi-t. and after he had retired to bed she went and found a bat-le coatainiag three or lour pennyworth f laulanum. The Following in•orni ig deceased iv-ked whether his s's-er hid si en anything in the closet, but did not mention whtt it was. D iceased's mind had been deranged for two raY4.—Th Coroner : Have you :akea any steps t) protect him P—Witness: Of course I have talked te him I could not do any more. I told him it was all non- sense he way be Was carrying on.— file Coroner: Did you consi ter he Was in ti.s ri mind?—Witness : We'l one day he would be as lively as possible, and another day he would his" all down." Continuing witness said deceased dis:marged his duties t his employers all right, and w..s getting on ve.'y well. He came to Frodsnam on the Sunday, and returned to Accringtua on the Tuesday.—The CJYOLIer at this point read the foßowing extract of a letter received from tse d -ceseed's e nployer :—" I am sorry to hear of the sad reed of your son, and still more sorry to think that he should tell you a lie without any reason."—The Coy Hoer to witneis : What was the lie ? —Witnese said wla a deceased came back to Frodsham on Wedne.day Ligtit he to:d his sister " My master has been swearing at me." Witness wrote to the deceased emp,oyer and asked him whether he had beta swearing at n.m, and the litter (produced) was the reply. The 14ter c outinued as f Mows :—" I did not sea your son , when he returned as I was out on other business, and I do not make a practice of swearing at my assistants. , Your son returned here after supper, about 10 30 which, I thi,dc, is too late for a boy. I spoke to him q He bade me good night and went to bed. On Wednesday about ten o'clock, he said he wanted to leave, so I told him to give the usual notice, and he would be at liberty to go the week following next Satur- day, and a porter came into the shop and said Walter had pawned his coat for 2s, and was going home by the 1-30 train. I have not seen or heard of thin till I got your letter this dinner time."—The Coroner(to witness): Yeu did not take any means to protect him when he has threatened so otten to commit suicide P—Witness : I have taken no means. but I have advised him. I told him it was all foolery for a young man like him to say a thirg of that sort. I told him his mind was affected.—Tae Coroner also subral!ted the following note writ en in black lead, which Was found in the coat of the deceased :—" Walter Brett is hear—(here)—Good bye, mother and sister, father and brothers. Please take me home. There is room in the glorified place for me. I can't stand it any longer."—Tbe Coron4 r : What did he mean by saying " I can't stand it any longer ?" Do you think it was the low wages, is. 61. a week ? Witness: I thought he was doing well. de had been at the business two years—at Runcorn and Accrington, and told me his master had started him shaving and was gone to start him hair cutting. Sarah Ellen Widders, sister of the deceased, said she had heard ner brother threaten to take his life on several oelasinns ; the last time being Monday afternoon. Sae was not ac are ta at he had ;me troable on his mind. He used to threaten to take his life wataout any cause.so far as she was a ware. Indeed, she Was getting so used to deceased making this statement a out doing away witii himself that. see to ok wry Ride notze of juror : Was it true that any girl threatened bun in any way ? Wit.ie s: Not that lam aw ire of.— ['he foreman : W.s he keepine c amp iny with sty- girl ? —Witness: I don't thill so.—Proceeding witness rated that de- ceased retuned on Wedneadar ev-ning at 7 o'clre k. She said in him" Hallo! What are Tod doing here 2" Be d "On, I've come back. He began swearieg at me becsuse I did not get back sooner." Deceased referred to hes master. Pulling his watch, handkerchief, and a few envelopes out ,f his pocket deceased pat them iin the sideboard, saying "I will go and drown myself" awl straightway went out by the backdoor.—rhe Ceroaer : Dain't you think that by taking his watch and p sp rs out of his pocket he intended to do it straight awes P—Witness : I did nrt. I went out to look for himand soon afterwards heard he had jumped into the river.</p>
        <p>EJward Boden, lock-keeper at Frodsham Locks, on the Weaver Canal, stated he was in the garden and saw the deceased piss along the towing path about 7 15 p.m. Shortly afterward.* h heard a whistle, and as he could not see anyone he le, ked in the opposite directinn. He saw deceased who was anout 300 yards away jump tut° the water. His first thought was that decemed be- ing a good swimmer had come there to have a bathe,but it seemed strange to him that he should come there while it was raining heavily, and he walked to the spot as quiAlly as he could. Be saw the deceased's jacket and cap lying on the wall, and called " Walter" twice, but, receiving no reeponse ran to his house for a boat hook and life hoik. With tne assistance of Allen Lovett and his vos-in-law he made a search for the body. Lovett recovered the br dy about six or seven minutes after the occurrenee.. Witness tried artificial respiration and sent for PC. Price and Dr. Burton their efforts however were of no avail. The whistle was like that of a steam packet. Deceased had been about the place twice be- fore, and about half•an-hour previously had called at witness's house to ask whether his son Ted was in. Toe Coroner said he thought there could be no doubt in the minds of the jury, thit the deceased committed suicide whilst he was suffering from temporary insanity. It seemed to him rather strange that this lad &amp;valid only be getting ls 61 a week. However, there was no evidence before them that he was dissatisfied with his situation. The oily evidence was that his mind was weak, an 4 that when he made these statements he had causs for it. Ile thought it was quite conclusive that he committed the rash act whilst of unsound mind.</p>
        <p>The Jury concurred, and retorned a verdict accord- ingly.</p>
