        <head>ALLEGED PERJURY BY</head>
        <p>ALLEGED PERJURY BY</p>
        <p>DISMISSAL OF THE CASE.</p>
        <p>At the Frodsham Petty S shone on Wedneelay, con- siderable interest was centred in a case in which John Thomas B-.o:h. barman at the Bridge Inn, Frodsham, was charged with that, he did falsely, wickedly,wilfully and corruptly commit perjure in evidence as a witness at Frodsham Sessions on the Mh of April last. Mr. Giles, Chester, pros' cuted, and Mr. A. Browne, Warrington, appeared for the defence. Mr. Giles //paned the case, fr ma which it would be gathered that the proceedings arose out of the case tried at the Frodsham 84/shone on the 26th ef April, when Mr. David Wilkin Brown, landlord of the Bridge Inn, Fred- sham, was charged with permitting drinking on his promises durin2 prohibited hours on the 19.:h of March. In the evideece of Both upan which tbe Beach had de- cidad and dianei+s, d e case, Booth swore that the men l'ollard and Dedi (two men implicated in the affair)had positively sta'ed to him that they same from near to the Highfield Tannery, Efaltoe-road, whereas the mea were now prepared to swear that Booth never was told any address on that date • but that on the 12th of March the previous 'Sunday when they atirwared at the Inn the first time, they told Booth they (male from Halton. Trius it would appear that the perjury consisted in Booth making that state- ment, whilst at the time to knew the men and was acquainted with th(m.—C derable evidence was sub.nitted to prove that 80, th had made the statement alleged to he perjury.—Mr. Browne in debnee s.id they admittel tha-. B )oth slaould make that statoment, and he would g.. into the hot and make it again in all truth- felneEs. But he stOm tte,l teat the assignation of per - jury was not materi,l to the is u and therefore no per- jury had been comaii ted. Flaying quoted his authority, the flPneh after a slight a ljaurnment decided that no prima facie case had ben made out. The charge was th?refore diunis ed.</p>
