        <p>Mr. S. B ighnuae, couiity coroner, neld an inquest at the St. Helens T wu Hall on Saturday, on the holy o Finny Brough, ai.ed eleven weeks, daughter of John Broagh, c lber. o 21. Wate-100-street, who was found dead in bed early on Friday morning, having apparently been suffocated M s Brough raid that on Thurbd'ly last they m v d from Et.as!ey Cross to Waterloo street. They went ti bed at a q rarer-past eleven that night_ She and h r husband an] the b by slept in one bed with another little daughter of two years old named Ruth. At half-pist four o,) Friday morning, when her husband got up t) go to work, he called her attention to the baby. She then found that it had slipped off the pillow, and was lying on it 4 face under the other child's back, being quite dead.—Tbo Coroner inquired how it name ab u•-, that so many slept in one bed. Mrs. Brough slid they had another bcd, but the little child got cut with ef in) glass at Pea.sley CrOF5 on the day of thn explosion, and she woulc) no.; sleep by herself.— The Coroner sal i that Inspector Goodall reported to him that be had oxarained the body, and it was a clear case of overiaing. Tne parents were quite respectablc,and were in crest distie34 et, r it.—The jury returned a ver- dict of" Acdent .1 sluff° atio-i.”—The coroner agreed. and .Aid it was evidnit theie had been no criminal negligence. The parents had had to move from Peasley C o aon. ace,unt of the expl ,s.OO, and they had to do the beat thev could under the circumstances. Mr. Brig- house call d the mother into Cie b x once more, an i weaned bet that the Wry ant himself were quite satir- fled tta,) thero was no blame attached to her,</p>
